  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11†",
    "prompt": "A well-known observation due to Euler is that the polynomial $P(x)=x^2+x+41$ takes on only prime values for $x\\in\\{0,1,2,\\ldots,39\\}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $P(40)$ is not prime. Hint: $P(40)$ is actually a square.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(40)=1681=41^2$, so it is not prime.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P(40)&=40^2+40+41\\\\P(40)&=40^2+2(40)+1\\\\P(40)&=41^2=1681\\end{align*}$$\nSince $41>1$, its square is composite.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p29-q11-proof-setup",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "In fact, no non-constant polynomial ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "P(x)",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " with integer coefficients can be prime for every integer ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ". We will prove this by way of contradiction. That is, we first suppose that ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "P(x)",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " is prime for every integer ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p29-q11-b-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Explain why the constant term ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "c",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " of the polynomial must be prime.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$c=P(0)$ is prime by the assumption.",
          "worked": "Since $0$ is an integer, the assumption says that $P(0)$ is prime. But $P(0)=c$, the constant term. Hence $c$ is prime.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "For every integer $x$, prove that $P(cx)$ is a multiple of $c$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(cx)=c\\bigl(a_nc^{n-1}x^n+\\cdots+a_1x+1\\bigr)$, an integer multiple of $c$.",
          "worked": "Write $P(t)=a_nt^n+a_{n-1}t^{n-1}+\\cdots+a_1t+c$, where all coefficients are integers.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(cx)&=a_n(cx)^n+a_{n-1}(cx)^{n-1}+\\cdots+a_1cx+c\\\\P(cx)&=c\\bigl(a_nc^{n-1}x^n+a_{n-1}c^{n-2}x^{n-1}+\\cdots+a_1x+1\\bigr)\\end{align*}$$\nFor integer $x$, the expression in brackets is an integer. Thus $P(cx)$ is a multiple of $c$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q11-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that $P(cx)=c$ for every integer $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(cx)=c$, since the prime $P(cx)$ is divisible by the prime $c$.",
          "worked": "For integer $x$, $cx$ is an integer, so $P(cx)$ is prime by the assumption. Part (c) shows that $c$ divides $P(cx)$. Since $c>1$, the only possibility is $P(cx)=c$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q11-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Define a new polynomial $Q(x)=P(xc)-c$. Explain why $Q(x)$ has infinitely many roots.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$Q(x)=0$ for every integer $x$, so every integer is a root.",
          "worked": "By (d), $P(xc)=c$ for every integer $x$. Therefore $Q(x)=P(xc)-c=0$ at every integer. There are infinitely many distinct integers, so $Q$ has infinitely many roots.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q11-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Deduce a contradiction by explaining why $P(x)$ must be constant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$Q$ is the zero polynomial, so $P(x)=c$ for all real $x$, contradicting the assumption that $P$ is non-constant.",
          "worked": "A nonzero polynomial has at most as many roots as its degree. Since $Q$ has infinitely many roots, it must be the zero polynomial. Hence $P(cx)=c$ for every real $x$. Since $c$ is prime and therefore nonzero, $cx$ ranges over all real numbers. Thus $P(t)=c$ for every real $t$, so $P$ is constant. This contradicts the assumption that $P$ is non-constant.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      }
    ]
  },
